course: dictionary
lesson: 983
title: Boasting and how it feels
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Boasting means talking too proudly about yourself, what you have or what you can do, to make others feel smaller. You can find the meaning in the clues around it. «Good friends let others finish speaking and share the fun instead of boasting.» Sharing the fun is the opposite of boasting, so boasting must be the opposite of sharing: showing off.
explain: It is fine to feel proud of something you have done. The problem starts when pride becomes showing off. Check with the swap test: put «talking too proudly about yourself» into the sentence in place of boasting. Does it still make sense? If so, you have found the right meaning.
explain: Boasting can be a noun too: «Boasting» is the act of talking like this. In stories, a boastful character often learns a lesson. The hare in «The Tortoise and the Hare» boasts about how fast he is, and the boasting costs him the race.
fact: Boasting is a verb (boast, boasts, boasted, boasting) and it can also be a noun.
fact: «Boasting» means talking too proudly about yourself to make others feel smaller.
fact: Phrases to know: «boast about», «stop boasting», and «nothing to boast about».
fact: «Nothing to boast about» means there is nothing good or impressive to be proud of.
fact: In lessons about friendship, boasting is seen as unkind or annoying, while being proud and showing off are different things.
exercise: «Good friends let others finish speaking and share the fun instead of boasting.» What does boasting mean here? | Telling a joke to make everyone laugh | *Talking too proudly about yourself to make others feel smaller | Listening very carefully | Asking questions about other people
exercise: «Nothing to boast about» means what? | Something to be very proud of | Something good to eat | *Nothing good or impressive to be proud of | A long and exciting story
exercise: «A good player wins without boasting, loses without sulking.» What kind of player is this? | Bossy and proud | *Humble and fair | Sulky and grumpy | Rude and loud
test: «The hare kept boasting about how fast he was.» What does boasting show? | Feeling sorry for others | Being shy and quiet | *Showing off in a way that makes others feel smaller | Running very fast
test: Which sentence shows a friend NOT boasting? | Sam kept saying he won every game | *Sam let Priya finish her story and then shared the fun | Sam bragged about his new bike | Sam told everyone how clever he was all day
exam: Ruby says, «Stop boasting!» What does she want her friend to do? | Go to bed early | Share a sweet with her | Sing a song louder | *Stop talking too proudly about themselves to make others feel small
